<binaryimage arch="x86:LE:16:Real Mode">
<!--
    kuna #1288: a DOS program linked with the 8087 emulator (DAZZLE.EXE,
    Turbo C) carries no x87 opcodes. The linker turns each WAIT+ESC pair into
    a software interrupt the emulator services: INT 34h+n stands for ESC
    D8h+n and is followed by the ModR/M and displacement, INT 3Ch by a byte
    holding the segment override (bits 6-7: DS SS CS ES) and the ESC opcode,
    and INT 3Dh is FWAIT. kuna decoded them as interrupts, so the operand
    bytes became garbage instructions: FLD1 (CD 35 E8) read as INT 35h and a
    near CALL to a phantom function.

    Independently assembled (nasm -f bin, org 0), each 9B Dx / 9B seg Dx /
    90 9B rewritten the way the emulator fixups rewrite them:
      0x00 start: calls fles(far ptr) and scale(3.0)
      0x25 scale: fld qword [bp+4]; fmul qword [k]; fld1; faddp;
                  fstp qword [res]; fwait
      0x40 fles:  les bx,[bp+4]; fld dword [es:bx]; fstp qword [res]; fwait
      0x53 segs:  fld dword [ds:bx]; fld dword [ss:bx]; fld qword [cs:k];
                  fld dword [es:di]; fwait
      0x68 k = 2.5, 0x70 res, 0x78 arg = 3.0

    Stage: P2 decode context (option fpuemu, default auto). A bytechunk has
    no MS-DOS executable for auto to read evidence from, so it decodes as
    off; the CLI test dos_fpuemu_cli covers auto on a synthetic MZ. Pass one
    is option fpuemu off: asserts 1-3 show the interrupts, the phantom call
    FLD1 decodes to, and INT 3Ch read as an interrupt. Pass two is option
    fpuemu on: asserts 4-6 are the float arithmetic, asserts 7-10 the four
    segment forms of INT 3Ch and assert 11 the emulated FWAIT.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x0">
